in the heart of the balkans  conservationists are fighting to save  what they say  is europe's last free-flowing river from  the headwaters to the mouth  the aos or viosa starts in the mountains  of northern greece sprints into southern  albania and pours into the adriatic  in greece much of the river is a  national park but in albania the  government has plans to dam it for  hydroelectric power  a europe-wide movement is pressing the  albanian government to declare the river  and its tributaries a national park  because this it says is an unparalleled  cradle of biodiversity  there is absolutely no systematic  research being done  so far so we don't even know about the  huge amount of biodiversity what we know  so far  is amazing you know scientists  a few years ago assessed the river in in  one place  only for four days and they found  40 species new to albania and two  species new to science  the conservationist movement recently  won albania's first major  environmental legal victory the  government planned some 40 dams along  the viosa and its tributaries  the first was to be built here at  kalivac flooding the valley behind me  but last september the government  cancelled it on environmental grounds  the consortium building the dam appealed  and in early june  it lost the court case that ruling means  a cascading system of dams down river  also cannot be built at the farming  village of bochem for example  a dam was to go up where this bridge now  stands  this farmer told me the community was  against it and local resistance  seems to have been key to defeating it  there was no public consultation done by  the authorities or by the construction  company  we stressed this point and the court  decided the entire bid process  should be cancelled if you look at when  the government invited competitive bids  and when construction began  it is clear that it was in a great hurry  for the past decade albania has  generated all its electricity from  hydroelectric dams  this gives the country energy autonomy  and its emissions free  but global warming is reducing river  flows and the capacity of dams  conservationists say sun and wind can  provide renewable energy without  destroying habitats  and what makes this habitat unique is  the vios's unfettered freedom to choose  its course  and flood in winter most people they  think  that they know what a river looks like  they have no idea  because we all grew up with channelized  with damned  rivers with diverted rivers albania is  not a wealthy country and aspires to a  european  standard of living but many local  residents see a more lucrative future in  alternative tourism and science than one  based on exploitation of nature  now that they've won a major battle for  that vision they're confident they can  prevail  john cerropolos al jazeera viosa
 

